      {
        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "2f1007c0-1aad-5f98-80ba-d526fa718df5",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Gabriela Mistral, Alan Sillitoe, Geoffrey Chaucer, Thomas Wyatt, Ivo Andrić, Saint-John Perse, Miguel Ángel Asturias, Pablo Neruda, George Seferis, Czesław Miłosz, Octavio Paz\n- Roles: poet-diplomats, angry young men\n- Works: Saturday Night and Sunday Morning\n- Awards: Nobel Prize in Literature, Author's Club First Novel Award\n\nFacts:\n- Alan Sillitoe was born on 4 March 1928 and died on 25 April 2010.\n- In the 1950s, Alan Sillitoe was regarded as one of the English writers called the \"angry young men,\" but he disliked that label.\n- In 1945, Gabriela Mistral became the first Latin American author to receive the Nobel Prize in Literature.\n- The Nobel Prize in Literature citation for Gabriela Mistral honored her lyric poetry as inspired by powerful emotions and as symbolizing the idealistic aspirations of the Latin American world.\n- Geoffrey Chaucer and Thomas Wyatt are among the best known poet-diplomats.\n- The category of poet-diplomats includes Nobel Prize in Literature recipients Ivo Andrić, Gabriela Mistral, Saint-John Perse, Miguel Ángel Asturias, Pablo Neruda, George Seferis, Czesław Miłosz, and Octavio Paz.\n- Saturday Night and Sunday Morning is the first novel by British author Alan Sillitoe.\n- Saturday Night and Sunday Morning won the Author's Club First Novel Award.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "bcf983d4-4081-5273-acce-115f65cd185f",
